<sect> matcalc (Revision 1.7 -- 09/09/98)


<p>The unit <tt/matcalc/ regroups routines for matrix operations. 
In many programs implementing adjustments using the least squares method,
matrices have to be dealt with. In <tt/matcalc.h/, some useful routines 
 for matrix operations are implemented.

<p>The library functions do not perform any checks for correct matrix
dimensions! If compiled with <tt/WITH_DEBUG/ defined, most 
functions will perform some checks to find programming
errors more easily.

<sect1> Matrix Numerical Type and Miscellaneous Macro Definitions

<p>All matrices are arrays of type <tt/double/. This is defined 
with the  macro <tt/MATTYP/ and should only be redefined, if you need
the library for calculating big matrices of another numerical type 
other than <tt/double/.

<sect2> macro definitions
<p>
<descrip>
<tag/ MATTYP/
   This macro defines the numerical type for all matrix operations
   in <tt/matcalc.h/. By default it is set to <tt/double/.
   If <tt/MATTYPE/ has to be redefined, you will need to 
   recompile the whole library to use your special version.

<tag/NEARLYZERO/
  This macro defines an iteration criterion for inverting
  matrices. It is set to <tt/1.0e-15/.

<tag/MAT_TRUE and MAT_FALSE/
  <tt/MAT_TRUE = 1/ and <tt/MAT_FALSE = 0/. These two macros 
  were introduced to make life easier and the code more readable.
  <em/Beware:/ in C everything <tt/!= 0/ is considered true!
</descrip>

<sect2> struct matrix
<label id="struct-matrix">
<p>The matrix structure type is used to store a matrix together with its
description and size definition. It contains the following members:

<p>
<tscreen><verb>
  struct matrix {
     char *name; 
     MATTYPE **mat; 
     MATTYPE *fast; 
     int row; int col; 
     int dim_row; int dim_col 
  }
</verb></tscreen>

<p><tt/char *name/. 
You may optionally assign a name to your matrix. 
This field is only processed by some I/O functions of the library. 
It may serve during the debugging stage of a program.

<p><tt><bf>MATTYPE **mat</bf></tt>.
Normal access to matrix. With this structure element you may
access a matrix element using a syntax like <tt/matr->mat[4][2]/.
Actually, all of the library functions access the matrix this way.

<p><tt><bf>MATTYPE *fast</bf></tt>.
Fast access to matrix. Through this structure element it is possible to
access the whole matrix as a single array of <tt/MATTYPE/. This might
prove useful for writing filters with fuzzy pointer usage. If you don't
know, what I'm talking about, don't use it!

<p><tt><bf>int row</bf></tt> and <tt><bf>int col</bf></tt> correspond to the 
 actual number of rows and columns in use. These values must never exceed
 <tt/dim_row/ and <tt/dim_col/ resp. 

<p><tt><bf>int dim_row</bf></tt> and <tt><bf>int dim_col</bf></tt> correspond to
allocation size of the matrix. These values are used during initialization (<tt/m_init/)
for memory allocation and never changed by any library function thereafter.The actual
dimension in use are stored in <tt/int row/ and <tt/int col/.


<sect1>Initializing and Freeing of a Matrix

<p>You should always operate with pointers to a matrix structure.
The matrix initialization routine will allocate all the needed
space, including the space for the structure itself.

<sect2>m_init
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct matrix * m_init (const int row, const int col)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function allocates the memory needed for a matrix
of the dimensions specified with <tt/row/ and <tt/col/.
It sets the <tt/dim_row/ and <tt/dim_col/ as well as
<tt/row/ and <tt/col/ members of the structure type. 
 All matrix elements are initialized with zeros. 
The return value is the pointer to the newly allocated
matrix or a NULL pointer, if the allocation was not successful.

<sect2>m_name
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_name (struct matrix *m,const char *newname)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function copies the string passed by <tt/newname/
to the <tt/name/ member of the matrix <tt/m/.

<sect2>m_init_name
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct matrix * m_init_name (const int row, const int col, const char *newname)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function allocates the memory needed for a matrix
of the dimensions specified with <tt/row/ and <tt/col/.
It copies the string passed by <tt/newname/
to the <tt/name/ member of the matrix <tt/m/.
It sets the <tt/dim_row/ and <tt/dim_col/ as well as
<tt/row/ and <tt/col/ members of the structure type. 
All matrix elements will be initialized with zeros. 
The return value is the pointer to the newly allocated
matrix or a NULL pointer, if the allocation was not successful.
(e.g. when there is no virtual memory left)

<sect2>m_free
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void m_free (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function properly frees all the memory space allocated by
an earlier call to <tt/m_init/ or <tt/m_initName/.

<sect2>m_resize
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_resize (stuct matrix *m,
	      const int row,
	      const int col,
	      const int set_zero)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This function resizes a matrix <tt/*m/ to number of columns <tt/col/ and number of rows <tt/row/. The return value for proper resizing is 0.


<sect1>Filling Elements of a Matrix

<p>With the following functions you may fill part or the whole of 
a matrix with a specific value. The return value is <tt/0/ upon
successful completion of the task. Actually, it always
succeeds or causes a <tt/SIGSEGV/ signal, unless <tt/WITH_DEBUG/
was defined at compile time of the library.

<sect2>m_fill
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_fill (struct matrix *m, const int row, const int col, const MATTYPE value)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function sets the matrix element at row <tt/row/ and
column <tt/col/ to the value <tt/value/. It is actually kind of obsolete,
except that it performs some paranoid checks on the validity of
<tt/row/ and <tt/col/ when <tt/WITH_DEBUG/ was defined
at compile time of the library.

<sect2>m_fill_row
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_fill_row (struct matrix *m, const int row, const MATTYPE value)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function  fills the row specified by <tt/row/ with
<tt/value/. It  performs some paranoid checks on the validity of
<tt/row/ when <tt/WITH_DEBUG/ was defined at compile time of the library.

<sect2>m_fill_col
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_fill_col (struct matrix *m, const int col, const MATTYPE value)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function  fills the column specified by <tt/col/ with
<tt/value/. It performs some paranoid checks on the validity of
<tt/row/ when <tt/WITH_DEBUG/ was defined at compile time of the library.

<sect2>m_fillsym
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_fillsym (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function assumes <tt/m/ to be an upper triangular matrix and
fills the lower triangle symmetrically.

<sect2>m_zero
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_zero (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function sets all matrix elements to zero. It is implicitly
called by <tt/m_init/ and <tt/m_init_name/

<sect2>m_zero_row
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_zero_row (struct matrix *m, int row)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function is similar to <tt/m_fill_row/, except that it
uses a faster algorithm to zero all matrix elements in the row
<tt/row/. Since it is not possible to use the same approach
for colums no function <tt/m_zero_col/ exists. It can be generated
by using <tt/m_fill_col/ with the value <tt/0/.

<sect2>m_unit
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_unit (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> This function sets all the diagonal matrix elements to one
and all other matrix elements to zero.

<sect2>m_set
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_set (struct matrix *m, MATTYPE value)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function sets all matrix elements to the value <tt/value/.

<sect2>m_random
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_random (struct matrix *m, MATTYPE fac)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function fills all matrix elements with random numbers.
The random numbers are generated by subsequent calls to <tt/rand/
declared in <tt/stdlib.h/. The random values lies in the range 
 <tt/[0, fac[/.


<sect1> Matrix Input-Output
<p>
The Common Library also provides tools to store and reread matrices
to files. These functions are somewhat provisional and yet to be debugged
completely.

<sect2>m_write
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_write (FILE *fpout, struct matrix *m, const int fmt1, const int fmt2,
	     int n, const int tr, const int title)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function prints a matrix <tt/m/ in ASCII format to the file <tt/fpout/.
The values of <tt/fmt1/ and <tt/fmt2/ define the output format, where <tt/fmt1/
 is the total length and <tt/fmt2/ the number of digits after the 
decimal point. Additional line breaks within a row are inserted after 
every  <tt/n/ values. (<tt/n = 0/ no additional line breaks).
When <tt/tr = MAT_TRUE/, the function writes the transposed
 matrix. When <tt/title = MAT_TRUE/, all columns and rows
are labelled properly. 

<sect2>m_writename
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void m_writename (FILE *fpout, struct matrix *m, 
	         int tr)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This function writes the name of the matrix <tt/*m/,
i.e. <tt/m->name/, to <tt/*fpout/. If <tt/tr/ is not equal zero, the
name gets the addition 'transposed'.

<sect2>m_writefmt
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_writefmt (FILE *fpout, struct matrix *m, const char * fmt, int n,
		const int tr, const int title)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> This function is similar to <tt/m_write/. Its prints a matrix 
<tt/m/ in ASCII format to the file <tt/fpout/. <tt/fmt/ represents a
format string for one element (e.g. <tt/"5.3lf"/). Additional  line breaks 
within a row  are inserted after every  <tt/n/ values. (<tt/n = 0/ no 
additional line breaks). When <tt/tr = MAT_TRUE/, the function writes the transposed
matrix. When <tt/title = MAT_TRUE/, all columns and rows are labelled properly.

<sect2>m_write_filled
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_write_filled (FILE *fpout, struct matrix *m, const int tr, MATTYPE nearlyzero)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This functions writes only the distribution of zero-nonzero elements in the
matrix <tt/m/. When <tt/tr = MAT_TRUE/, it writes the information in transposed 
form. All elements with an absolute value smaller than <tt/nearlyzero/ are considered
zero. This routine may be useful during the debugging stage of a program.

<sect2>m_writebin
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_writebin (FILE *fpout, struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function writes the matrix <tt/m/ to the binary file <tt/fpout/ which 
must already be open. (This allows to write more than one matrix to the same
file).

<sect2>m_writebin2
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_writebin2 (char * fname, struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This function writes the matrix <tt/m/ to the binary file
<tt/fname/. Unlike the function <tt/m_writebin/, this function opens
and closes the file <tt/fname/.

<sect2>m_readbin
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_readbin (FILE *fpin, struct matrix **m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function is the counterpart of <tt/m_writebin/. It 
reads the matrix <tt/m/ from the binary file <tt/fpin/ which 
must already be open.(This allows to read sequentially  several matrices 
from the same file). If the matrix <tt/m/ is a 
<tt/NULL/ pointer memory is allocated, if not the routine  supposes that the 
matrix is already initialized. In this case an error is returned
(negative return value) if the matrix to read does not fit in the allocated space. 

<sect2>m_readbin2
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_readbin2 (char *fname, struct matrix **m)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This function is similar to <tt/m_readbin/, but unlike <tt/m_readbin/,
this function opens and closes the file <tt/fname/.

<sect2>m_readascii
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_readascii( char *fname, struct matrix **m, int row2read, int col2read)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> This function reads a matrix from an ASCII file. The values <tt/row2read/
and <tt/col2read/ specify the dimension of the matrix. If one of these two values
is zero, the routine counts the total number of values on the file and calculates
by divison the corresponding missing dimension. In this case the file is read
twice. Line delimiters are ignored and element after element read (row fill).
If the matrix <tt/m/ is a <tt/NULL/ pointer memory is allocated, if not the 
routine supposes that the matrix is already initialized. In this case an error is 
returned (negative return value) if the matrix to read does not fit in the 
allocated space. 



<sect1>Arithmetic Operations on Matrices

<p>The Common Library only provides some basic matrix operations. In all routines
the result matrix has to be initialzed externaly and a return value <tt/0/ indicates
a successfull completion of the operation.

<sect2>m_add
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_add (struct matrix *m1, struct matrix *m2, struct matrix *m_res)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function adds matrix <tt/m1/ to matrix <tt/m2/. The result is
writen to matrix <tt/m_res. (m_res = m1 + m2). m_res/ must be initialized.


<sect2>m_sub
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_sub (struct matrix *m1, struct matrix *m2, struct matrix *m_res)
</verb></tscreen>
This function substracts matrix <tt/m2/ from matrix <tt/m1/. The result is
writen to matrix <tt/m_res. (m_res = m1 - m2). m_res/ must be initialized.


<sect2>m_mult
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_mult (struct matrix *m1, const int m1tr, struct matrix *m2,
	    const int m2tr, struct matrix *m_res)
</verb></tscreen

<p> This function multiplies matrix <tt/m1/ with matrix <tt/m2/. The
result matrix <tt/m_res/ must be initialized. If the parameter <tt/m1tr
= MAT_TRUE/ the transposed matrix of <tt/m1/ is used. If the parameter
 <tt/m2tr = MAT_TRUE/ the transposed matrix of <tt/m2/ is used. This 'transpose'
flag allows to use the transposed matrix in a multiplication 
without having to physically generate this transposed matrix using <tt/m_trans/. 
 
<p>Example: <tt/m_mult(m1, MAT_TRUE, m2, MAT_FALSE, m_res)/ for  <tt/m_res = m1^T * m2/. 

<sect2>m_multx
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_multx (struct matrix *m, MATTYPE scalar, struct matrix *m_res)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> This function is the scalar multiplication of the matrix <tt/m/ by
the scalar value <tt/scalar/. The result matrix <tt/m_res/ must be
initialized.


<sect2>m_trace
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
MATTYPE m_trace (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> The return value of this function is the trace of the matrix <tt/m/.


<sect2>m_trans
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_trans (struct matrix *m, struct matrix *m_res)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> This function fills the matrix <tt/m_res/ with the transposed version
of the original matrix <tt/m/. The matrix <tt/m_res/ must be initialized.

<sect2>m_det
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_det(struct matrix *m, MATTYPE *det)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> This function calculates the determinant <tt/det/ of a quadratic matrix.
Note that the routine needs to create a copy of the matrix. A nonzero
return value indicates an error.

<sect2>m_atpa1
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_atpa1 (struct matrix *a, struct matrix *p, struct matrix *atpa)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> This function calculates a normal equation matrix <tt/atpa/ from
a design matrix <tt/a/ of dimension (<tt/n x m/) and a weighting matrix 
<tt/p/ of dimension (<tt/n x n/). The result matrix is of dimension 
(<tt/m x m/) and must be initialized. <tt/atpa = a(transposed) * p * a/. 
<bf/Attention/ : the matrix <tt/p/ is considered to be  diagonal and
stored in a matrix of dimension <tt/1 x n/. Element p(i,i) is stored in
<tt/p[0][i]/.

<sect2>m_pivot
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_pivot (struct matrix *m, int row, int col)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> This function pivots the element <tt/m[row][col]/ in the matrix <tt/m/. 
The pivoting corresponds to one step in an inversion using Gauss elimination.

<sect2>m_inv
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_inv (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function calculates the inverse of the matrix <tt/m/. The original
matrix is overwritten (replaced by its inverse). The algorithm works with
a Gauss elimination (pivoting). The function returns <tt/0/ on successful
completion of the inversion. In the opposite case the return value is the defect
of the matrix. <bf/Attention/: The routine may fail for
not positive definite matrices, because pivot elements are only choosen
along the diagonal. (MC This should be changed in a later version MC). 
(Note that all normal equation matrices are positive definite).

<sect2>m_cholesky_decomposition
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_cholesky_decomposition (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function calculates the upper triangular matrix associated to
the Cholesky inversion. The upper part of the original matrix is 
overwritten with the resulting triangular matrix. (The lower part
 excluding the diagonal still helds the original elements).

<sect2>m_choleskyinv
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_choleskyinv (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function calculates the inverse of the matrix <tt/m/. The original
matrix is overwritten (replaced by its inverse). The algorithm is based
on a Cholesky decomposition and only works with positive definite matrices.
It is faster than <tt/m_inv/. The function returns <tt/0/ on successful
completion of the inversion. In the opposite case the return value is the defect
of the matrix. (Note that all normal equation  matrices are positive definite).

<sect2>m_separate_sym
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_separate_sym(struct matrix *m, struct matrix *sym, struct matrix *asym)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function decomposes the matrix m in a sum af two matrices, a symmetrical
one <tt/sym/ and an asymmetrical one <tt/asym/. <tt/(m = sym + asym)/. Note that 
a <tt/NULL/ pointer for <tt/sym/ or <tt/asym/ means that this matrix will
not be calculated. 

<sect1> Administrative and Miscellaneous Operations on Matrices

<p>This last chapter regroups all administrative tools. In addition all tools which
do not fit in one of the previous chapter are included here.

<sect2>m_WriteMatInfo
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void m_WriteMatInfo (struct matrix *m, int i)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This function writes the name and the number <tt/i/, the dimensions
and the memory allocation of the matrix <tt/m/ to STDERR.

<sect2>m_error
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void m_error (const char *procname, 
	     struct matrix *m1,
	     struct matrix *m2,
	     struct matrix *m3,
	     int *row, int *col)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This function writes information about the matrices <tt/m1/, <tt/m2/ and
<tt/m3/ using function <tt/m_WriteMatInfo/ and the variables <tt/row/
and <tt/col/ to STDERR, provided that the pointers do not point to NULL. The
function exits with status EXIT_FAILURE.

<sect2>m_checkdimension
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void m_checkdimension (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This function compares the <tt/dim_row/ with <tt/row/ and <tt/dim_col/
with <tt/col/ of the matrix <tt/m/. If <tt/row/ or <tt/col/ are greater
than <tt/dim_row/ or <tt/dim_col/ respectively, the function calls
<tt/m_error/. 

<sect2>m_copy
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_copy (struct matrix *source, struct matrix *target)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function stores a copy of the matrix <tt/source/ in matrix <tt/target/.
The matrix <tt/target/ must be initialized.

<sect2>m_sym_check
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
MATTYPE m_sym_check (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function checks the elements <tt/m[i][j]/ versus <tt/m[j][i]/. In the case
of a symmetric matrix the difference between these elements should be zero. The
function returns the maximal absolute value of the difference encountered.

<sect2>m_add_block
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_add_block (struct matrix *m, struct matrix *block, int row, int col)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> This function adds a submatrix <tt/block/ to the matrix <tt/m/. The starting
point in matrix <tt/m/ is given by <tt/row/ and <tt/col/. This means e.g. that
the element <tt/block[i][j]/ is added to <tt/m[i+row][j+col]/. The result is 
the updated matrix <tt/m/.

<sect2>m_pick_block
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_pick_block (struct matrix *m, struct matrix *block, 
                  int row, int col, int drow, int dcol)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> This functions picks a submatrix <tt/block/ from the original matrix <tt/m/.
<tt/row/ and <tt/col/ are the starting point in the matrix <tt/m/.
 <tt/drow/ and <tt/dcol/ are the number of rows and columns to be extracted. 
Hence they correspond to the dimension of the picked block. (<tt/block[i][j] = 
m[i+row][j+col]/). Note that the matrix block must already be initialized.

<sect2>m_del_row
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_del_row (struct matrix *m, int row)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function deletes the row <tt/row/ from the matrix <tt/m/.
Note that the memory allocation dimensions (<tt/dim_row x dim_col/) 
of the matrix <tt/m/ are not changed.

<sect2>m_del_col
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_del_col (struct matrix *m, int col)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function deletes the column <tt/col/ from the matrix <tt/m/.
Note that the memory allocation dimensions (<tt/dim_row x dim_col/) 
of the matrix <tt/m/ are not changed.

<sect2>m_pushup
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_pushup (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> This function shifts all rows of the matrix <tt/m/ up. (row(i) becomes row(i+1)).
The last row is replaced by the first one. (row(last) becomes row(0)).

<sect2>m_pushdown
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_pushdown (struct matrix *m)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> This function shifts all rows of the matrix <tt/m/ down. (row(i) becomes row(i-1)).
The first row is replaced by the last one. (row(0) becomes row(last)).

<sect2>m_max_col
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_max_col (struct matrix *m, const int col, MATTYPE *max, int *row)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>The function searches the maximal value <tt/max/ in the column <tt/col/ of 
 matrix <tt/m/. <tt/row/ corresponds to the  row where the maximum value 
was encountered.

<sect2>m_min_col
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_min_col (struct matrix *m, const int col, MATTYPE *min,int *row)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>The function searches the minimal value <tt/max/ in the col <tt/col/ of 
 matrix <tt/m/. <tt/row/ corresponds to the  row where the maximum value 
was encountered.

<sect2>m_max
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_max (struct matrix *m, MATTYPE *max, int *row,int *col)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>The function searches the maximum value <tt/max/ in matrix <tt/m/. 
<tt/row/ and <tt/col/ give its corresponding location.

<sect2>m_min
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_min (struct matrix *m, MATTYPE *min, int *row, int *col)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>The function searches the minimal value <tt/max/ in matrix <tt/m/. 
<tt/row/ and <tt/col/ give its corresponding location.

<sect2>m_absmax
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_absmax (struct matrix *m, MATTYPE *max, int *row, int *col)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>The function searches the maximum absolute value <tt/max/ in matrix <tt/m/. 
<tt/row/ and <tt/col/ give its corresponding location.

<sect2> m_polynom_approx
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_polynom_approx (struct matrix *data, 
		      const int  col_x,   const int col_y,  
		      const int  col_w,   const int col_app,
		      const int col_res,  const int row_beg,
		      const int row_end,  const int degree,
		      MATTYPE x0, MATTYPE *dummy,
		      struct matrix *coeff, struct matrix *qxx, MATTYPE *s0)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> This function performs a polynomial approximation based on the
following formula using a least square adjustment :
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
 y = a0*(x-x0) + a1*(x-x0)^2 + a2*(x-x0)^3 + ....
</verb></tscreen>

<p>Data are supposed to be organized columnwise in the 
matrix <tt/m/. The number of the column containing the x-values is
given by <tt/col_x/.  
A negative value for <tt/col_x/ means that the x-value is taken
identical to the index. 
The number of the column containing the y-values is given by
<tt/col_y/. The number of the column containing the observation (x)
weight is given by col_w. A negativ value for <tt/col_w/ sets the weight to
1 for all obs. The approximated y-values are stored in column
<tt/col_app/ and the residual of the approximation in column
<tt/col_res/. A negative value for <tt/col_app/ or <tt/col_res/ means
that these values will not be stored at all. Note that 
 the following simple relationship helds : residual = y - y(approx).
<p><tt/row_beg/ and <tt/row_end/ allows a windowing on the rows of the matrix.
Only rows within this window are used for the calculation. A negative
value for <tt/row_beg/ and <tt/row_end/ sets the window to the full
length of the data.
<p><bf/Attention/: <tt/degree/ defines the number of coefficients of the 
polynomial to be calculated. e.g. for a polynomial approximation of degree 1 
(linear approximation) the parameter <tt/degree/ has to be put to <tt/2/. 
(2 coefficients : a constant term and a linear term).
<p><tt/x0/ allows to shift the x-values (see formula). If the y-array is not
complete a dummy value <tt/dummy/ allows to discard corresponding rows. Note
that a value <tt/dummy = NULL/ means that no dummy check is performed.  
<p>The result of the approximation : the coefficient of the approximation <tt/coeff/ 
(dimension <tt/degree x 1/), the cofactor matrix  <tt/qxx/ (dimension <tt/degree x degree/)
the covariance of unit weight <tt/s0/. The two output matrices <tt/coeff/ and <tt/qxx/ must
be initialized.

<sect1>Non-free routines

<p>For convience routines from numerical recipies have been slightly modified
for compatibilty with <tt/struct matrix/ of <tt/matcalc/. These routines are 
held separately in files called <tt/nfcalcmat.c/ resp. <tt/nfcalmat.h/.

<sect2> m_jacobi<em/x/
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int m_jacobi1(struct matrix *a, struct matrix *eval, struct matrix *evec, int *nrot)
int m_jacobi2(struct matrix *a, struct matrix *evec, int *nrot)
</verb></tscreen>

<p>Both functions perform an eigenvalue decomposition of a symmetric matrix <tt/a/.
with <tt/A = R D R^T/, where <tt/D/ is a diagonal matrix containing the 
eigenvalues and <tt/R/ is a rotation matrix.
<p> Identical to both routines : The symmetrical input matrix <tt/a/, the output
matrix containing the eigenvectors <tt/evec/ in form of a rotation matrix. <tt/nrot/
is the total number of individual rotations during iteration. Both routines return
<tt/0/ on successful completion otherwise <tt/-1/.
<p> Difference between <tt/m_jacobi1/ and <tt/m_jacobi2/ : In <tt/m_jacobi2/ the 
original matrix <tt/a/ is replaced by the diagonal eigenvalue matrix, whereas
in <tt/m_jacobi1/ the original matrix <tt/a/ is preserved. The resulting eigenvalues
are stored in the vector <tt/eval/. <bf/Attention:/ <tt/eval/ is a vector of
dimension <tt/1 x dim/.






